==Battleground==
*3 [[Doge Dark]]s spawn after 13.33 seconds<sup>400f</sup>, delay 16~18.67 seconds<sup>480f~560f</sup>.
*1 [[Mesocosmocyclone]] spawns after 33.33 seconds<sup>1,000f</sup>.
*When the base reaches 99% HP:
**1 [[Henry]] spawns as the boss.
**Infinite [[Doge Dark]]s spawn, delay 36.67~43.33 seconds<sup>1,100f~1,300f</sup>.
**2 [[Mr. Mole]]s spawn, delay 13.33~20 seconds<sup>400f~600f</sup>.
**4 [[Mesocosmocyclone]]s spawn, delay 16~20 seconds<sup>480f~600f</sup>.

==Strategy==
This stage can be incredibly frustrating to play, due to the limited cash, the Mesocosmocyclones' warp and insanely high DPS and the Henry and Mr Mole's procs. One thing to note is that Mesocosmocylcones' warp can put your Meatshields/Attackers in the blindspot of both Henry and the moles, which can cause them to attack even when your stack isn't in their standing range.

However, you can use this to your advantage. By actively trying to get a few Manic Erasers/Royal Guards warped onto Henry and Mr Mole, you can stall them at their base and lure the cyclone to your base. From there, it's just spamming high DPS units. [[Hip Hop Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Can Can Cat]], [[Swimmer Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Fishman Cat]], [[Cossack Cat (Rare Cat)|Royal Guard Cat]] and even [[Wheel Cat (Rare Cat)|Chill Cat]] can burst Mesocosmocylcone incredibly quickly, and [[Surfer Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Seafarer Cat]] can give you windows to send out these units and prevent warps. The plan isn't foolproof, but whenever a new Mesocosmocyclone spawns, the same strategy can be used.

It is likely that Henry and Mr. Mole will push up to the base around when the third or fourth cyclone spawns. When your attackers get frozen, send out [[Bahamut Cat (Special Cat)|Awakened Bahamut Cat]] to finish off the rest of the cyclones. Mr Mole can potentially still chip Bahamut, but with some luck, it will help Bahamut to rebound and deal more damage. Once the cyclones are dead, the stage is basically won.

==Trivia==
*Like all [[Unearthed Artifacts]] stages, Patience Prevails' Japanese name references an idiom. In this case, the name Ishi no Ue ni Sanpun (石の上に三分, "Three Minutes on a Stone") is a play on the idiom ''ishi no ue ni mo sannen'' (石の上にも三年, "Three years on a stone"), which means that no matter how difficult something is, it can eventually be achieved with enough patience.

